\subsection{Channel B: arrangement anomalies}
On the three test scenarios with rearranged regions the rarity was at chance: its average precision was @TC_ARR_R@ (the prevalence is @TC_PREV@), and similarity weighting did not change that (@TC_ARR_A@), as it must be when the distribution of the embeddings is untouched. The directional disorder reached @TC_ARR_B@ (95\% interval @TC_ARR_B_CI@; paired gain over channel A @TC_ARR_GAIN@; Table~\ref{tab:tc}). The simple distance of a site to its eight neighbors was equally good (@TC_ARR_CNB@), the isotropic disorder $D$ reached @TC_ARR_D@, and a local manifold statistic (the distance of a site from the plane spanned by the two leading principal directions of its $5\times5$ neighborhood) @TC_ARR_MAN@. The directional form is thus not a better detector of rearranged regions than a simple baseline; the difference lies in what else the statistics respond to. In the feature-outlier scenarios (iii), whose background has walls between domains, channel B alone found thin anomalies (scattered sites @TC_B_SC@, bars @TC_B_BAR@) and ignored compact blocks (@TC_B_B3@ and @TC_B_B6@), whose interior is ordered; its tail index there was @TC_TAIL_B_BLK@, against @TC_TAIL_CNB_BLK@ for the neighbor distance and @TC_TAIL_D_BLK@ for the isotropic disorder, which respond to every wall. On the images and the MyUncle lattices channel B alone was weak (@TC_B_H@, @TC_B_D@, @TC_B_R@ and @TC_B_RD@).

