\subsection{Label-free choice of the channel}
On the development scenarios the cohort-level selection had the highest fitness of the seven fusion rules ($F=$ @TC_DEV_FUS@, against @TC_DEV_A@ for channel A). On the fifteen test scenarios it reached $F=$ @TC_F_FUS@ (95\% interval @TC_F_FUS_CI@), against @TC_F_A@ for channel A, @TC_F_R@ for the unsmoothed rarity and @TC_F_M@ for the fixed mean; the paired gain over channel A was @TC_GAIN_A@ and over the unsmoothed rarity @TC_GAIN_R@. The gain came from the scenarios with rearranged regions, and nothing was lost on the ten earlier scenarios (mean AP @TC_OLD_FUS@ against @TC_OLD_A@; paired difference @TC_OLD_DIFF@). The tail index chose channel A in @TC_NA@ of the @TC_NF@ test cohorts that contain only feature anomalies (the exception was one cohort of bars, where both channels work) and channel B in all @TC_NB@ cohorts with rearranged regions; the requirement $T_B>2\times1.88$ was never the deciding condition. The directional form mattered for the choice: with the neighbor distance in place of $B_i$ the selection reached $F=$ @TC_F_FCNB@ (paired difference to the directional version @TC_D_FCNB@), because the walls of the block scenarios gave it a heavy tail and it was chosen wrongly there (AP @TC_CNB_B3@ and @TC_CNB_BND@ for $3\times3$ and boundary blocks, against @TC_FUS_B3@ and @TC_FUS_BND@), and with the isotropic disorder $F=$ @TC_F_FD@ (@TC_D_FD@). The site-wise rules were below the selection: the maximum of the standardized scores @TC_F_ZMAX@ (it failed on the DINOv2 images, @TC_ZMAX_D@), the maximum of the ranks @TC_F_RMAX@, and the tail-weighted maxima @TC_F_TW@.

Three limits were measured. (a) A cohort-level choice follows one channel. In the mixed cohorts it chose channel B and reached an AP of @TC_MIX_FUS1@ and @TC_MIX_FUS2@ over both kinds of anomaly (channel A: @TC_MIX_A1@ and @TC_MIX_A2@), but taken by kind, the rearranged sites were found by channel B (@TC_MIX_B_ARR1@ and @TC_MIX_B_ARR2@) and the feature outliers only by the rarity (@TC_MIX_R_OUT1@ and @TC_MIX_R_OUT2@ unsmoothed; channel B @TC_MIX_B_OUT1@ and @TC_MIX_B_OUT2@): when both kinds occur together, the two maps must be read side by side (Fig.~\ref{fig:channels}). (b) In the smooth background of the mixed cohorts similarity weighting lowered the AP of scattered feature outliers from @TC_MIX_R_OUT2@ to @TC_MIX_A_OUT2@, because coherent patches of mildly rare normal sites are reinforced; channel A is not uniformly better than the unsmoothed rarity. (c) A first attempt, fixed blends of the ranks of channel A and of the isotropic disorder, had been selected on thirteen of the development scenarios ($F=$ @G1_DEV_BLEND@ against @G1_DEV_A@) and did not beat channel A on the corresponding test scenarios (@G1_TEST_BLEND@ against @G1_TEST_A@); the directional statistic and the tail index were designed after that, so thirteen of the fifteen test scenarios had been used once before.

\subsection{What did not help}
\label{sec:neg}
\emph{Order gates.} The development-selected gates did not beat ordinary similarity on the ten test scenarios: the gate on compatibility reached $F=$ @F_OGCOS@ and the gates on the resultant length and on $H_\chi$ @F_OGVMF@, against @F_SIMW@ for the untuned similarity-weighted rule (@D_OGCOS_SIMW@ and @D_OGVMF_SIMW@); the last two gave the same scores to within 0.01, as the monotone relation of $H_\chi$ and $R_i$ requires. Local order was useful as a channel of its own, not as a gate on the rarity, and the $d$-dimensional entropy is reported as a formal result. \emph{Other rules.} The one-sided rule matched no smoothing (@D_ONE_NONE@ in $F$), and so did the nucleation-and-growth rule ($F=$ @F_NGH@; @D_NGH_NONE@; @D_NGH_SIMW@ against similarity weighting). The retention of the top-3\% sites under the fixed mean correlated with the effect of the fixed mean over @N_COH@ cohorts (Spearman $\rho=$ @CG_RHO@, $p=$ @CG_P@), but a choice between the fixed mean and no smoothing based on it reached a mean AP of @CG_CHOICE@ on the test cohorts against @CG_NONE@ for no smoothing and @CG_SIMW@ for similarity weighting. \emph{Search.} Among the @OG_N@ rule configurations the frozen fixed mean ranked @OG_FRANK@th on the development fitness (@OG_FF@; optimum @OG_OPT@). With @OG_BUDGET@ evaluations and @OG_REP@ repetitions, random search found a configuration in the top 5\% in @OG_RN5@ of the runs, the GaaR strategies in @OG_G5@ (greedy), @OG_R5@ (restart) and @OG_P5@ (population), and in the top 1\% in @OG_RN1@ against @OG_G1@, @OG_R1@ and @OG_P1@: GaaR was not better than random search here, because many configurations of the similarity-weighted family are nearly equivalent, and its champion ($F=$ @F_GAAR@) was no better than the untuned rule. An earlier search of the scoring configuration itself (360 configurations; repository) gave a gain on fresh scenarios of @OLD_DIFF@ with a 95\% interval of @OLD_CI@, which includes zero.

