\section{Limitations and future work}
The lattices and the images are simulated and were written by the author of the method (the simulation is MyUncle's and its parameter sets are not fitted to measurements; the generators have Gaussian noise, and a permutation is an idealized arrangement anomaly that changes nothing but the arrangement); nothing here shows that the method finds anything in an experimental system. Development and test data come from the same generator families, @TEST_UNITS@ independent units per test scenario are few, and the choice among rules uses development labels. The tail index compares two numbers per cohort: it cannot serve a cohort that contains both kinds of anomaly, and when neither channel has a tail (the DINOv2 images, @TC_TAIL_A_DINO@ against @TC_TAIL_B_DINO@) the choice rests on the default. Channel B needs a background that is locally ordered; where normal sites are themselves disordered it has nothing to measure. Novelty is not a defect: a rare or disorganized state may be a rare preparation or a known feature that is merely uncommon. The rarity score was compared with other detectors (isolation forest, local outlier factor, nearest-neighbor and Mahalanobis distance) under the earlier fixed smoothing (repository): a smoothed Mahalanobis baseline scored higher on the development images, so the rarity is not claimed to be the best channel-A detector. Planned: validation on public data with logical anomalies \citep{Bergmann2022} and on experimental micrographs with independently established defects, blinded expert review, more units per scenario, a site-wise fusion that serves mixed cohorts, and other filters of the bilateral and non-local-means families as baselines.

\section{Conclusions}
Rarity and local order are complementary label-free channels on a lattice of embeddings. Rarity, aggregated with similarity weights instead of a fixed window, finds what is unusual; the directional local disorder, a quantity of the MyUncle lattice model, finds ordinary embeddings in the wrong arrangement, to which rarity is blind (average precision @TC_ARR_R@ against @TC_ARR_B@). A label-free tail index chose channel A in the test cohorts with feature anomalies and channel B in those with rearranged regions, and raised the lower-tail average precision over fifteen independent test scenarios from @TC_F_A@ to @TC_F_FUS@ (paired interval of the gain @TC_GAIN_A_CI@) without loss on the scenarios that contain only feature anomalies. Cohorts with both kinds of anomaly still need both maps, and all evidence is simulated; experimental validation is the next step.

